    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "A study was conducted to determine differences in study designs and how they effect rates of specific reported symptoms in oral contraceptive users. Five hundred women were contacted by phone; 1 group was contacted twice during each contraceptive cycle and half were asked if they experienced any symptoms or side effects (general query), while a second set were asked if they experienced specific symptoms from a prepared list. These results were compared to women who were only contacted once/contraceptive cycle, but again 1 group was asked a general query and 1 group was asked a specific query. It was shown that more side effects were reported when patients were contacted twice/contraceptive cycle as opposed to once/cycle. In addition, high rates of side effects were reported when patients were asked about specific rather than general symptoms. Differences in study designs and data collection may affect the interpretations of reported results."
    }
